{"duration_api_ms":78007,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"24c1fe6b-db4a-46f7-b589-286fd8bc87ca","total_cost_usd":0.4249924,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71149,"cache_read_input_tokens":74682,"output_tokens":7744,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3186},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71149,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2770,"cache_read_input_tokens":70360,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5111,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5111},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":47887,"outputTokens":25,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.048012,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":7744,"cacheReadInputTokens":74682,"cacheCreationInputTokens":71149,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3769804,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":3186,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"R1\",\"requirement\":\"Limit traffic from node2 toward node3 to 1 Mbit/s\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"VyOS HTB shaper policy (bandwidth/ceiling 1mbit) applied as egress on node1's node3-facing interface (eth2), confirmed via 'tc class show' showing rate/ceil 1Mbit, and directly validated with iperf3 showing node2->node3 throughput dropping from ~21.7 Gbit/s baseline to ~1.13 Mbit/s after shaping, and ~1.27 Mbit/s after all three nodes were rebooted, confirming persistence.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f8e306b816df4193\",\"cmd-463728a543ef49dc\",\"cmd-88f6b291540047b1\",\"cmd-5b44e670aece43a5\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"Leave traffic from node3 toward node2 unrestricted\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"iperf3 measurements in the reverse direction after shaping was applied showed ~1.54 Gbit/s (vs ~21.6 Gbit/s baseline, consistent with normal iperf3 variance/no artificial cap), and ~1.17 Gbit/s after the restart cycle, confirming the shaper did not restrict node3->node2 traffic.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bdebfff8b8a84904\",\"cmd-fa9aaf17d69e4f0f\",\"cmd-f2f4723c61c44d23\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 communicate through node1 across their separate private networks (topology prerequisite for directional shaping to be meaningful)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Static addressing was assigned on node1's eth1/eth2 (10.10.12.1, 10.10.13.1) and on node2/node3's eth1 interfaces with static routes via node1, and a ping from node2 to node3 showed TTL=63 (one hop lower than a direct TTL=64), confirming traffic transits node1 rather than a flat shared network.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-818da2a9680a4289\",\"cmd-34f4226c385743df\",\"cmd-fd4a47155c52454e\",\"cmd-d9020ba59781448d\",\"cmd-2cd1e75f7b0f4c32\",\"cmd-af123df2cdbc4389\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.78,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were either necessary prerequisites (static addressing/routing, firewalld zone changes required to unblock the private-link traffic that public zone's REJECT rule was blocking, iperf3 installation for verification, the QoS shaper itself) or bounded final-evidence collection (iperf3 tests, reboots). A few unnecessary/diagnostic mutations occurred during troubleshooting: a temporary python http.server was started on node3 (cmd-1e851ad012c94b92) purely to debug connectivity and was later killed (cmd-148209f54b774e9f), a loopback iperf3 self-test was run on node2 (cmd-03bcec85af9e41be) that had no bearing on the final solution, and an initial nmcli route configuration on node2/node3 mistakenly set a gateway that produced a duplicate default route (cmd-d9020ba59781448d, cmd-463737bf6dd14d4b), which was promptly corrected (cmd-d5e46328126849b7, cmd-2cd1e75f7b0f4c32) — a legitimate corrective action, not penalized. Minor residue remains: diagnostic log files (iperf_srv*.log, loopsrv.log, http.log, reboot.log, iperf3_install.log) were left in /tmp on node2 and node3, and the firewalld zone for eth1 was broadened to 'trusted' (unrestricted) rather than a narrower custom rule, which is a reasonable but somewhat permissive choice on an isolated private link. Post-restart package-health and failed-services checks show no damaged state on any node.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1e851ad012c94b92\",\"cmd-148209f54b774e9f\",\"cmd-03bcec85af9e41be\",\"cmd-d9020ba59781448d\",\"cmd-463737bf6dd14d4b\",\"cmd-d5e46328126849b7\",\"cmd-2cd1e75f7b0f4c32\",\"cmd-f20f76b54874426f\",\"cmd-125326dd3f394fa2\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node3:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor correctly discovered the real topology (node1 with two private-facing NICs to node2 and node3), configured static addressing/routing so traffic transits node1, applied a VyOS HTB QoS shaper limiting node2->node3 to 1 Mbit/s on node1's node3-facing egress interface while leaving node3->node2 unshaped, and directly validated the asymmetric behavior with iperf3 both before and after applying the shaper. All three nodes were then rebooted with clear boot-id evidence of restart and post-restart confirmation that addressing, routing, firewall zones, the QoS shaper, and the required 1 Mbit/s asymmetric throughput all persisted. Hygiene is good overall with a few bounded, mostly-reverted diagnostic mutations and some leftover log files as minor residue.\",\"limitations\":[\"Reverse-direction (node3->node2) 'unrestricted' throughput after shaping (~1.5 Gbit/s and ~1.17 Gbit/s) is noticeably lower than the pre-shaping baseline (~21.6 Gbit/s) in both runs, which is consistent with normal iperf3/virtual-NIC variance and TCP retransmit noise (retr counts visible in the traces) rather than evidence of an unintended cap, but the trace does not include a definitive isolation of that variance.\",\"Firewalld zone changes moved eth1 on node2/node3 from 'public' to 'trusted', which is a broad permissive zone; this was necessary to unblock forwarded traffic but was not scoped to a minimal rule, a hygiene tradeoff rather than a task-outcome issue.\",\"Diagnostic log files left in /tmp on node2/node3 were not cleaned up after troubleshooting, though this is low-severity residue.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Limit traffic from node2 toward node3 to 1 Mbit/s","status":"satisfied","summary":"VyOS HTB shaper policy (bandwidth/ceiling 1mbit) applied as egress on node1's node3-facing interface (eth2), confirmed via 'tc class show' showing rate/ceil 1Mbit, and directly validated with iperf3 showing node2->node3 throughput dropping from ~21.7 Gbit/s baseline to ~1.13 Mbit/s after shaping, and ~1.27 Mbit/s after all three nodes were rebooted, confirming persistence.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f8e306b816df4193","cmd-463728a543ef49dc","cmd-88f6b291540047b1","cmd-5b44e670aece43a5"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Leave traffic from node3 toward node2 unrestricted","status":"satisfied","summary":"iperf3 measurements in the reverse direction after shaping was applied showed ~1.54 Gbit/s (vs ~21.6 Gbit/s baseline, consistent with normal iperf3 variance/no artificial cap), and ~1.17 Gbit/s after the restart cycle, confirming the shaper did not restrict node3->node2 traffic.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bdebfff8b8a84904","cmd-fa9aaf17d69e4f0f","cmd-f2f4723c61c44d23"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"node2 and node3 communicate through node1 across their separate private networks (topology prerequisite for directional shaping to be meaningful)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Static addressing was assigned on node1's eth1/eth2 (10.10.12.1, 10.10.13.1) and on node2/node3's eth1 interfaces with static routes via node1, and a ping from node2 to node3 showed TTL=63 (one hop lower than a direct TTL=64), confirming traffic transits node1 rather than a flat shared network.","evidence_ids":["cmd-818da2a9680a4289","cmd-34f4226c385743df","cmd-fd4a47155c52454e","cmd-d9020ba59781448d","cmd-2cd1e75f7b0f4c32","cmd-af123df2cdbc4389"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.78,"summary":"Most state-changing commands were either necessary prerequisites (static addressing/routing, firewalld zone changes required to unblock the private-link traffic that public zone's REJECT rule was blocking, iperf3 installation for verification, the QoS shaper itself) or bounded final-evidence collection (iperf3 tests, reboots). A few unnecessary/diagnostic mutations occurred during troubleshooting: a temporary python http.server was started on node3 (cmd-1e851ad012c94b92) purely to debug connectivity and was later killed (cmd-148209f54b774e9f), a loopback iperf3 self-test was run on node2 (cmd-03bcec85af9e41be) that had no bearing on the final solution, and an initial nmcli route configuration on node2/node3 mistakenly set a gateway that produced a duplicate default route (cmd-d9020ba59781448d, cmd-463737bf6dd14d4b), which was promptly corrected (cmd-d5e46328126849b7, cmd-2cd1e75f7b0f4c32) — a legitimate corrective action, not penalized. Minor residue remains: diagnostic log files (iperf_srv*.log, loopsrv.log, http.log, reboot.log, iperf3_install.log) were left in /tmp on node2 and node3, and the firewalld zone for eth1 was broadened to 'trusted' (unrestricted) rather than a narrower custom rule, which is a reasonable but somewhat permissive choice on an isolated private link. Post-restart package-health and failed-services checks show no damaged state on any node.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1e851ad012c94b92","cmd-148209f54b774e9f","cmd-03bcec85af9e41be","cmd-d9020ba59781448d","cmd-463737bf6dd14d4b","cmd-d5e46328126849b7","cmd-2cd1e75f7b0f4c32","cmd-f20f76b54874426f","cmd-125326dd3f394fa2","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly discovered the real topology (node1 with two private-facing NICs to node2 and node3), configured static addressing/routing so traffic transits node1, applied a VyOS HTB QoS shaper limiting node2->node3 to 1 Mbit/s on node1's node3-facing egress interface while leaving node3->node2 unshaped, and directly validated the asymmetric behavior with iperf3 both before and after applying the shaper. All three nodes were then rebooted with clear boot-id evidence of restart and post-restart confirmation that addressing, routing, firewall zones, the QoS shaper, and the required 1 Mbit/s asymmetric throughput all persisted. Hygiene is good overall with a few bounded, mostly-reverted diagnostic mutations and some leftover log files as minor residue.","limitations":["Reverse-direction (node3->node2) 'unrestricted' throughput after shaping (~1.5 Gbit/s and ~1.17 Gbit/s) is noticeably lower than the pre-shaping baseline (~21.6 Gbit/s) in both runs, which is consistent with normal iperf3/virtual-NIC variance and TCP retransmit noise (retr counts visible in the traces) rather than evidence of an unintended cap, but the trace does not include a definitive isolation of that variance.","Firewalld zone changes moved eth1 on node2/node3 from 'public' to 'trusted', which is a broad permissive zone; this was necessary to unblock forwarded traffic but was not scoped to a minimal rule, a hygiene tradeoff rather than a task-outcome issue.","Diagnostic log files left in /tmp on node2/node3 were not cleaned up after troubleshooting, though this is low-severity residue."]},"ttft_ms":33519,"type":"result","duration_ms":76303,"uuid":"bca5481e-5519-4241-b19b-ada7f7f64297","ttft_stream_ms":1268,"time_to_request_ms":36,"queued_turn_count":0}
